A failed `CreateProcessW` came back as an `anyhow` message only: the command line and the working directory in Rust
debug text (doubled backslashes, the NUL terminators, `Some(...)`), then the OS error's text, so a caller could neither
read the OS error nor say what failed in plain words. The error now wraps the `io::Error` itself
(`downcast_ref::<io::Error>()`) under the same "CreateProcessW ... failed" context, and its alternate display (`{:#}`)
reads as before. ngterm-pty reads it to name the program as written beside the OS error (a pane whose shell profile's
program is gone says so), see `crates/ngterm-pty/src/windows/mod.rs`.

ngterm's own. Remove when portable-pty keeps a `CreateProcessW` failure's `io::Error`. Its test: ngterm-pty's
`a_missing_program_is_refused_naming_it_as_written`.

